1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,700
You ask a chatbot to set up a meeting,

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:05,400
and it writes you a nice message about setting

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:08,100
up meetings. But what if it simply set up

4
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:10,800
the meeting? That is the difference between a chatbot

5
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:13,500
and an AI agent. By the end of this

6
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:16,200
video, you will know what an AI agent is,

7
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:18,300
and the loop that makes it work.

8
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:22,410
A chatbot answers. You ask a question, it writes

9
00:00:22,410 --> 00:00:25,719
a reply, and then it waits for you. An

10
00:00:25,719 --> 00:00:29,029
AI agent works toward a goal. You tell it

11
00:00:29,029 --> 00:00:32,339
what you want done, and it decides the steps,

12
00:00:32,339 --> 00:00:35,649
uses tools to take them, checks what happened, and

13
00:00:35,649 --> 00:00:38,224
keeps going until the job is finished.

14
00:00:39,024 --> 00:00:42,453
Think of a capable personal assistant on their first

15
00:00:42,453 --> 00:00:45,883
day. You say, set up a meeting with Amina

16
00:00:45,883 --> 00:00:49,312
next week. A good assistant does not reply with

17
00:00:49,312 --> 00:00:52,742
advice. They check your calendar, check Amina's calendar, pick

18
00:00:52,742 --> 00:00:56,172
a time, and send the invite. They plan, act,

19
00:00:56,172 --> 00:00:59,601
look at the result, and adjust. An AI agent

20
00:00:59,601 --> 00:01:01,887
is software that does the same.

21
00:01:02,687 --> 00:01:06,114
Every agent has four parts. First, a model, usually

22
00:01:06,114 --> 00:01:09,541
a large language model. This is the brain that

23
00:01:09,541 --> 00:01:12,967
reads the situation and decides what to do next.

24
00:01:12,967 --> 00:01:16,394
Second, tools. These are things the agent can use,

25
00:01:16,394 --> 00:01:19,820
like a calendar, a web search, or code it

26
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:23,247
can run. Third, memory. It keeps track of what

27
00:01:23,247 --> 00:01:26,673
it has done so far. And fourth, a goal

28
00:01:26,673 --> 00:01:30,100
and rules, which say what to achieve and what

29
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:31,242
is off limits.

30
00:01:32,042 --> 00:01:35,583
Remember the waiter from our API video? Tools work

31
00:01:35,583 --> 00:01:39,125
the same way. Each tool is reached through an

32
00:01:39,125 --> 00:01:42,667
API. The agent sends a request, reads the answer,

33
00:01:42,667 --> 00:01:46,209
and decides what to do next. A calendar tool,

34
00:01:46,209 --> 00:01:49,750
a search tool, a code tool, an email tool.

35
00:01:49,750 --> 00:01:53,292
The agent can only do what its tools allow.

36
00:01:54,092 --> 00:01:57,388
Now the important part: the loop. An agent repeats

37
00:01:57,388 --> 00:02:00,683
three steps. Think: the model looks at the goal

38
00:02:00,683 --> 00:02:03,979
and what has happened so far, and decides the

39
00:02:03,979 --> 00:02:07,275
next action. Act: it uses a tool to take

40
00:02:07,275 --> 00:02:10,570
that action. Observe: it reads the result and adds

41
00:02:10,570 --> 00:02:13,866
it to its memory. Then it loops back to

42
00:02:13,866 --> 00:02:17,162
think again. This continues until the goal is done,

43
00:02:17,162 --> 00:02:20,091
or until the agent needs to ask you.

44
00:02:20,891 --> 00:02:24,698
Let's follow a real example. The goal: set up

45
00:02:24,698 --> 00:02:28,504
a meeting with Amina next week. Think: I need

46
00:02:28,504 --> 00:02:32,311
free times for both of us. Act: it asks

47
00:02:32,311 --> 00:02:36,118
the calendar tool for your free times. Observe: Tuesday

48
00:02:36,118 --> 00:02:39,924
at ten and Wednesday at two are free. Think:

49
00:02:39,924 --> 00:02:43,731
now I need to check Amina's calendar. Act: it

50
00:02:43,731 --> 00:02:47,537
asks the calendar tool for Amina's free times. Observe:

51
00:02:47,537 --> 00:02:51,344
only Wednesday at two works for both. Think: so

52
00:02:51,344 --> 00:02:55,151
book Wednesday at two. Act: it creates and sends

53
00:02:55,151 --> 00:02:58,957
the invite. Observe: the invite was sent. The goal

54
00:02:58,957 --> 00:03:01,918
is done, and the agent tells you.

55
00:03:02,718 --> 00:03:06,231
You may already see agents at work. Coding assistants

56
00:03:06,231 --> 00:03:09,744
that write code, run it, and fix errors. Support

57
00:03:09,744 --> 00:03:13,257
assistants that look up your order and process a

58
00:03:13,257 --> 00:03:16,770
refund. Research helpers that search many pages and summarize

59
00:03:16,770 --> 00:03:17,941
what they find.

60
00:03:18,741 --> 00:03:21,880
Agents are powerful, but they are not magic. They

61
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:25,020
can misunderstand a goal, use a tool the wrong

62
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:28,160
way, or keep going down the wrong path. So

63
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:31,300
well designed agents get only the permissions they need,

64
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:34,439
ask for your approval before risky actions like sending

65
00:03:34,439 --> 00:03:37,579
money or deleting files, and keep a log you

66
00:03:37,579 --> 00:03:40,719
can check. Treat an agent like a new assistant:

67
00:03:40,719 --> 00:03:42,114
helpful, but worth supervising.

68
00:03:42,914 --> 00:03:46,725
Let's recap. One: an agent works toward a goal,

69
00:03:46,725 --> 00:03:50,537
not just a reply. Two: it runs a loop

70
00:03:50,537 --> 00:03:54,348
of think, act, and observe, using tools and memory.

71
00:03:54,348 --> 00:03:57,313
Three: it needs limits and human oversight.

72
00:03:58,113 --> 00:04:01,155
The brain of every agent is a large language

73
00:04:01,155 --> 00:04:04,197
model. In the next video, we open it up

74
00:04:04,197 --> 00:04:07,240
and see how it predicts the next word. If

75
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:10,282
this helped, subscribe, and comment with the concept you

76
00:04:10,282 --> 00:04:13,324
want explained next. See you in the next one.

